#
# Competency question: "Under which policy do two remediation options fail to
# compare at all, and on what does the disagreement rest?"
#
# An incomparability is not a tie and not an absent edge: it is the POSITIVE
# verdict that this policy names no exchange rate between two criteria pointing
# opposite ways. The query therefore demands the structure that MAKES it one —
# two observations registered by the SAME context, over the same pair, each
# preferring what the other disprefers — and only then reads the positive
# gmeow:incomparableWith relation standing over them.
#
# That structural demand is what keeps the question apart from the strict-order
# one: the fastest-restore context also compares rollback and the hotfix, but
# its order is coherent and holds no opposed pair, so it contributes no row.
# Over the contextual-recommendation scene exactly ONE row comes back — hotfix
# and rollback, under minimise-regulatory-exposure — and the honest answer to
# "which should we pick?" under that policy is that a human must decide, and
# should know that is what they are doing.

PREFIX gmeow: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/>

SELECT DISTINCT ?context ?a ?b WHERE {
    ?context gmeow:registersObservation ?forA , ?forB .
    ?forA gmeow:observationPrefers ?a ;
          gmeow:observationDisprefers ?b .
    ?forB gmeow:observationPrefers ?b ;
          gmeow:observationDisprefers ?a .
    ?a gmeow:incomparableWith ?b .
}
ORDER BY ?context ?a ?b
